CHAPTER I
THE PROBLEM AND ITS BACKGROUND


Introduction


Under the Department of Education (DepEd) K to 12 Technical-Vocational-Livelihood (TVL) track and the Technical Education and Skills Development Authority (TESDA) National Certificate II (NC II) standards, Computer Systems Servicing (CSS) serves as a foundational specialization designed to prepare secondary students for entry-level ICT careers. The curriculum mandates that learners master core competencies in installing and configuring computer systems, setting up computer networks, configuring servers, and maintaining or repairing IT infrastructures. However, as educational institutions navigate increasingly complex digital landscapes, a distinct gap has emerged between student mastery of routine, linear technical tasks and their ability to perform advanced diagnostic troubleshooting during non-routine system failures.
In many technical laboratories, instructional emphasis remains heavily anchored on static computer assembly, standard software installation, and physical cable crimping. While Grade 11 CSS students frequently achieve high marks in these structured environments, they experience severe cognitive bottlenecks when confronted with ambiguous, multi-layered fault scenarios such as power-on self-test (POST) beep failures, operating system driver corruptions, system stop codes, and network packet loss. Rather than applying systematic fault isolation techniques, learners often resort to ad-hoc, unstructured trial-and-error practices or premature hard drive reformatting.  
The challenge of advanced troubleshooting in Technical-Vocational Education and Training (TVET) is rooted in both pedagogical and structural limitations. Empirical evidence across Senior High School classrooms in Region IV-A confirms that Computer Systems Servicing instruction is severely constrained by static laboratory conditions and a shortage of authentic system failure scenarios, which prevents learners from developing systematic troubleshooting resilience (Bascuguin & Generoso, 2026). In secondary school laboratories, computer hardware is typically maintained in static, operational states to protect scarce institutional equipment. This creates what educators describe as the Clean Lab paradox: students are repeatedly taught how to construct functional systems, but are rarely given opportunities to diagnose dynamically corrupted or broken environments.
This research addresses the systemic need for a structured modeling approach to technical diagnostics. According to Padilla (2026) from Laguna State Polytechnic University, incorporating systematic problem-solving approaches and step-by-step diagnostic procedures significantly enhances students' ability to analyze, test, and resolve technical faults in computer systems 
Objectives of the Study
The primary objective of this research is to evaluate the unstructured troubleshooting habits of Grade 11 Computer Systems Servicing students and formulate a standardized, model-based diagnostic framework grounded in SYSMOD principles.
1. To determine the baseline level of unstructured troubleshooting practices and self-perceived technical competency among Grade 11 Computer Systems Servicing students across advanced hardware diagnostics, operating system recovery, and network fault isolation.
2. To analyze the diagnostic procedures, decision-making patterns, cognitive fatigue factors, and qualitative procedural errors encountered by Grade 11 CSS students during non-routine system failures.
3. To improve, and validate a Standardized SYSMOD-based Diagnostic Framework tailored for Grade 11 CSS laboratory instruction to transition learners from trial-and-error habits to structured fault isolation.
Statement of the Problem
1. What is the baseline level of unstructured troubleshooting practices and self-perceived technical competency among Grade 11 Computer Systems Servicing students across advanced hardware diagnostics, operating system recovery, and network fault isolation?
2. How do Grade 11 Computer Systems Servicing students execute diagnostic workflows during non-routine technical failures, and what qualitative procedural gaps, cognitive fatigue, and logical errors hinder their systematic fault isolation?
3. What suggestions and feedback on Standardized SYSMOD-based Diagnostic Framework can be use to improve structured model-based troubleshooting?
Theoretical Framework
This research is anchored exclusively on SYSMOD – The Systems Modeling Toolbox: Pragmatic MBSE with SysML (3rd Edition) authored by Tim Weilkiens (2016, 2020). SYSMOD is an established methodology in Model-Based Systems Engineering (MBSE) that utilizes Systems Modeling Language (SysML) to conceptualize, analyze, and architect complex, multi-layered systems.
In the context of Computer Systems Servicing, SYSMOD provides a robust theoretical foundation for transforming technical diagnostics from informal guessing into structured engineering analysis. SYSMOD models an IT infrastructure as an interconnected system composed of distinct hardware, operating system, device driver, and network communication subsystems. Key SYSMOD principles applied in this study include System Context & Boundary Modeling, State Machine & Behavior Modeling, Requirement & Interface Traceability, and Structural Decomposition.
Conceptual Framework
The conceptual framework adopts the Input-Process-Output (IPO) model to illustrate the research flow and data integration. The framework systematically links the student baseline conditions to the mixed-methods evaluation, culminating in the proposed instructional output.
  
  
  



Scope and Delimitation of the Study
This study focuses strictly on evaluating unstructured troubleshooting practices and developing a model-based diagnostic framework for Grade 11 TVL-ICT Computer Systems Servicing (CSS) students enrolled at the National College of Science and Technology (NCST) in Dasmariñas City, Cavite.
The scope is delimited to advanced technical competencies defined under TESDA CSS NC II standards, specifically covering hardware diagnostics (POST beeps, PSU voltage testing), operating system recovery (system event log analysis, bootloader restoration, driver conflict resolution), and network path isolation. The study excludes commercial electronics repair storefronts, non-CSS academic tracks, Grade 12 students, and third-party commercial service centers.
Significance of the Study
The findings and instructional outputs of this research provide substantial benefits to key educational stakeholders:
Grade 11 CSS Students: Gains a structured, logical methodology for diagnosing complex technical breakdowns, reducing cognitive frustration and eliminating reliance on trial-and-error guessing.
ICT Teachers & Instructors: Obtains an empirical, standardized diagnostic guide and pedagogical tool to enhance student analytical thinking during laboratory troubleshooting exercises.
NCST School Administration: Receives evidence-based recommendations for modernizing TVL-ICT laboratory instruction and optimizing equipment usage to mitigate hardware wear caused by improper diagnostics.
Future Researchers: Establishes a solid empirical benchmark for subsequent research exploring mixed-methods diagnostic evaluations and systems modeling applications in vocational education.


Definition of Terms
Advanced Troubleshooting: The systematic process of isolating, identifying, and resolving non-routine, multi-layered failures in computer hardware, operating system software, and network communications.
Unstructured Troubleshooting: An ad-hoc, informal approach to technical repair characterized by random component swapping, unsystematic guessing, and trial-and-error without logical fault isolation.
SYSMOD: The Systems Modeling Toolbox developed by Tim Weilkiens, providing a pragmatic Model-Based Systems Engineering (MBSE) methodology to analyze systems via SysML.
6-Step Diagnostic Method: A standard 6-stage industry troubleshooting sequence consisting of: (1) Identify problem, (2) Establish theory, (3) Test theory, (4) Plan & implement solution, (5) Verify system functionality, and (6) Document findings.
Clean Lab Paradox: The instructional limitation where school computer laboratories maintain hardware in strictly working conditions, preventing students from experiencing dynamic system breakdowns.
Mean Time to Repair (MTTR): The average duration required to diagnose, repair, and restore a malfunctioning computer system to full operational status.
Diagnostic Task Observation Protocols: systematically monitor actions and capture procedural errors.
Empirical evidence: Evidence based on real world experience.
