CHAPTER II
REVIEW OF RELATED LITERATURE AND STUDIES
Competency Baselines and Advanced Troubleshooting Deficits
The mandate of the K to 12 Technical-Vocational-Livelihood track in the Philippines is to equip senior high school learners with job-ready technical competencies certified under TESDA National Certificate II frameworks. In recent national evaluations of TVL-ICT graduates, researchers consistently note a bifurcation between routine task mastery and advanced diagnostic capability. Studies by Arrabaca and Habil (2025) assessing Grade 12 ICT students specializing in Computer Systems Servicing revealed that while learners achieved high academic ratings in routine tasks—such as computer assembly (68% high honors) and local area network setup (72% highest honors)—their performance declined significantly when evaluated on server configuration and diagnostic maintenance. Their self-assessed preparedness dropped to lower bounds when confronted with unplanned system failures, emphasizing the urgent need for targeted diagnostic support.
Similarly, research by Pelayo et al. (2025) investigating student efficiency across technical training programs highlighted that while student satisfaction and procedural efficiency remain high for standardized tasks (assembling hardware mean = 4.50; preparing installers mean = 4.43), efficiency is heavily constrained by student inability to navigate non-routine software conflicts and hardware faults independently. Furthermore, Lozaga and Mananquil (2024) identified that institutional readiness across secondary schools is frequently challenged by gaps in advanced instructional materials and modern diagnostic tools, leaving students reliant on basic operating guides rather than comprehensive troubleshooting strategies.
Pedagogical Obstacles and the Clean Lab Paradox
A recurring theme across vocational education literature is the structural friction between laboratory preservation and authentic technical practice. In a comprehensive mixed-methods report from Batangas, Bascuguin and Generoso (2026) documented a significant perception gap between TVL teachers and school administrators regarding practical skills instruction. While teachers reported frequent implementation of problem-based learning and laboratory simulations, school heads rated these practice-intensive activities significantly lower due to resource constraints and monitoring gaps. The study emphasized that authentic technical mastery requires repeated, feedback-rich exposure to authentic failure scenarios—a condition rarely realized in standard school computer labs.
This instructional limitation is widely recognized as the Clean Lab paradox. Because school laboratories operate under tight budgets and limited spare inventory, computer systems are maintained in static, functional configurations. As noted by Dounas-Frazer and Lewandowski (2017) in their analysis of technical troubleshooting pedagogy, when students are trained exclusively on working equipment, they develop a linear mental model that assumes system stability. When exposed to real-world faults—such as intermittent power drops, corrupted registry hives, or driver conflicts—students experience cognitive overload and fall back on exhaustive, unstructured trial-and-error. Furthermore, as highlighted in DepEd TVL instruction guides, teachers frequently encourage students to format and reinstall operating systems to save time, effectively bypassing the critical root-cause analysis stage.
Pragmatic Systems Modeling (SYSMOD) in Technical Instruction
To bridge the gap between linear instruction and complex technical diagnostics, educational researchers advocate for the integration of structured systems frameworks. Tim Weilkiens (2016, 2020) formulated SYSMOD (The Systems Modeling Toolbox) to provide a pragmatic, light-weight Model-Based Systems Engineering (MBSE) approach using Systems Modeling Language (SysML). SYSMOD organizes complex technical systems into explicit requirements, structural blocks, state machines, and behavioral interactions. Applying SYSMOD to computer systems servicing enables students to visualize a PC not as a monolithic box, but as a set of interacting subsystems with explicit state dependencies.
Empirical research on simulation software and structured frameworks in vocational instruction demonstrates significant benefits. Kaysi (2025) demonstrated that integrating virtualized troubleshooting software and structured state diagrams significantly improved technical student problem-solving speed and diagnostic confidence. When learners utilize systems modeling tools—such as state-transition tables and signal flow diagrams—they learn to hypothesize root causes before touching hardware components. This systematic mental mapping directly mitigates cognitive fatigue and eliminates damaging trial-and-error practices.
Synthesis of the Study
The reviewed literature underscores three fundamental realities in Senior High School TVL-ICT education: (1) student technical competency is strong in routine assembly but highly vulnerable in advanced diagnostics; (2) traditional laboratory environments foster a Clean Lab paradox where students rarely experience authentic, dynamic system breakdowns; and (3) introducing Model-Based Systems Engineering frameworks like SYSMOD equips learners with the logical tools necessary to conduct systematic fault isolation. By synthesizing these insights, this capstone study positions SYSMOD not merely as an engineering tool, but as a practical instructional lens for secondary technical education.
Justification and Gap Bridged
While previous Philippine studies have examined general computer literacy (Boyon et al., 2019), teacher readiness (Lozaga & Mananquil, 2024), and workplace immersion preparedness (Arrabaca & Habil, 2025), virtually no research has specifically operationalized SYSMOD MBSE principles to address advanced troubleshooting deficits among Grade 11 CSS students. Prior research predominantly relies on purely quantitative surveys that capture self-perceived confidence rather than observing actual procedural diagnostic errors. This study bridges this empirical gap by employing a Convergent Mixed Methods design that pairs quantitative competency surveys with qualitative diagnostic task observations, establishing a contextualized Standardized Diagnostic Framework for Senior High School TVL-ICT instruction.
