CHAPTER III
METHODOLOGY
Research Design
This study employs a Convergent Mixed Methods Research Design (also known as a parallel convergent design). In this design, quantitative and qualitative data are collected concurrently within the same timeframe, analyzed independently, and subsequently merged during the interpretation phase to provide a comprehensive evaluation of the research problem.
The quantitative strand utilizes a descriptive survey design to measure student self-perceived competency levels, frequency of unstructured troubleshooting practices, and baseline skill ratings across advanced hardware, operating system, and network domains. Concurrently, the qualitative strand utilizes a diagnostic task observation protocol and focus group discussions (FGD) to capture real-time procedural errors, logical bottlenecks, and cognitive struggles experienced by students during simulated breakdown scenarios. Integrating both strands allows statistical trends to be directly explained by qualitative diagnostic behaviors.
Research Locale and Respondents
The study will be conducted at the Institute of Industrial Research and Training (IIRT) Senior High School Department of the National College of Science and Technology (NCST), located in Dasmariñas City, Cavite. NCST is a premier technical-vocational educational institution equipped with specialized TVL-ICT computer laboratories.
The target population comprises Grade 11 Technical-Vocational-Livelihood (TVL) ICT students specializing in Computer Systems Servicing (CSS) during the School Year 2025–2026. Grade 11 students represent the ideal respondent group as they are actively engaged in core CSS competency modules prior to their Grade 12 work immersion and national assessment.
Sampling Technique
The research utilizes Purposive Sampling (a non-probability sampling technique) to select respondents who possess direct exposure to CSS core subjects. The quantitative sample size will consist of approximately 50 to 75 Grade 11 CSS students enrolled across active laboratory sections. For the qualitative strand, a sub-sample of 10 to 12 students exhibiting varied diagnostic performance levels will be purposively selected to participate in structured focus group discussions and observed troubleshooting tasks.
Research Instruments
Data collection will be executed using two primary research instruments designed specifically for this study:
1. Quantitative Survey Questionnaire: A structured, 4-point Likert scale instrument adapted from TESDA CSS NC II competency standards and advanced troubleshooting indicators. The questionnaire measures student frequency of unstructured practices (1 = Never, 4 = Always) and perceived competency across hardware diagnostics, OS recovery, and network path isolation (1 = Not Competent, 4 = Highly Competent). The instrument will undergo expert content validation and pilot testing to establish internal reliability (target Cronbach alpha >= 0.70).
2. Qualitative Diagnostic Observation & FGD Guide: A structured observation checklist and open-ended interview guide used during simulated system breakdown tasks. The instrument records student diagnostic sequences, time elapsed per step, reliance on trial-and-error guessing, premature reformatting tendencies, and verbalized cognitive struggles.
Data Gathering Procedure
The data gathering process will follow strict ethical and administrative protocols:
1. Administrative Clearance: Formal permission letters will be submitted to the NCST IIRT-SHS Vice Principal, Research Adviser, and Department Heads to secure institutional approval for laboratory testing.
2. Informed Consent: Written informed consent forms will be distributed to student participants (and parent/guardian consent for minors), explicitly detailing research objectives, voluntary participation, and data privacy protections under Republic Act No. 10173 (Data Privacy Act of 2012).
3. Quantitative Survey Administration: The quantitative survey questionnaire will be administered during regular CSS laboratory schedules, ensuring a 100% retrieval rate.
4. Qualitative Task Observation & FGD: Purposively selected student sub-groups will undergo observed troubleshooting exercises on simulated breakdown PCs, followed immediately by semi-structured focus group interviews.
Statistical and Qualitative Data Treatment
Collected data will be organized, coded, and analyzed using appropriate quantitative and qualitative analytical tools:
1. Weighted Mean & Standard Deviation: To compute overall student competency levels and measure the frequency of unstructured troubleshooting habits across indicators.
2. Percentage Distribution: To summarize demographic distributions and respondent profile characteristics.
3. Qualitative Thematic Analysis: Qualitative interview transcripts and observation log notes will be analyzed manually using 6-phase thematic analysis (Swain & Chen, 2024) to identify recurring procedural traps, cognitive bottlenecks, and logical errors.
4. Mixed Methods Integration Matrix: Quantitative mean scores and qualitative thematic categories will be side-by-side converged in a joint display matrix to formulate the finalized Standardized SYSMOD-based Diagnostic Framework.
